<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemais Theron</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ptolemais of the Hunts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemais Theron, 'Ptolemais of the Hunts', was a marketplace on the African coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> founded, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, by Eumedes, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> sent there to support the hunting of elephants. The early Ptolemies, cut off from Indian elephants, sought African ones for war; the animals served at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a> but proved unstable and were eventually abandoned. Unlike most such stations Ptolemais had enough fertile land to sustain a town, but by the mid-1st century CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> describes it as having no harbour. Its site remains unidentified: Arqiqo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suakin">Suakin</a> and Trinkitat have all been proposed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek was the language of administration, diplomacy and trade, and sizeable Greek communities lived in Adulis and <b>Ptolemais Theron</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aksum">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Aksum</a></li></ul></section>
